  <!-- SEO vs GEO -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>🚀 SEO vs. GEO: The Big Shift</h2>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <h4>📚 Remember the Library Analogy?</h4>
      <p><strong>SEO</strong> = Getting your book on the shelf (list of links).</p>
      <p><strong>GEO</strong> = Getting the librarian to <em>read your book out loud</em> (AI answer).</p>
    </div>

    <h3>The Fundamental Difference</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Feature</th>
          <th>SEO (Traditional)</th>
          <th>GEO (AI Search)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Goal</strong></td>
          <td>Rank #1 in links</td>
          <td>Be quoted in the answer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>User Sees</strong></td>
          <td>10 blue links</td>
          <td>A paragraph written by AI</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Success</strong></td>
          <td>User clicks link</td>
          <td>AI cites your website</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Analogy</strong></td>
          <td>Being on the menu</td>
          <td>Being the chef's recommendation</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="success-box">
      <h4>💡 Why Coders Win at GEO</h4>
      <p>AI understands <em>code</em> better than marketing text. Your ability to add Schema and structure gives you a huge advantage!</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Facts Over Fluff -->
  <section id="facts">
    <h2>📊 Pillar 1: Facts Over Fluff</h2>

    <h3>What AI Loves vs. What AI Ignores</h3>
    <p>AI models are trained to find <strong>authoritative information</strong>. They hate vague marketing slogans.</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>❌ Fluff (AI Ignores)</th>
          <th>✅ Facts (AI Quotes)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>"We are the best!"</td>
          <td>"10 years experience, 500+ students"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>"Our lessons are amazing!"</td>
          <td>"95% pass rate on IELTS exams"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>"Contact us for more info!"</td>
          <td>"3-step method proven over 5 years"</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="question-box">
      <h4>🤔 Socratic Question 1</h4>
      <p>You ask AI: <em>"What is the best way to learn English?"</em></p>
      <p><strong>Website A:</strong> "We are the best English school! Click here to learn more!"</p>
      <p><strong>Website B:</strong> "Research shows 30 minutes of daily practice improves fluency by 40%. Waseem's School uses a 3-step coding-based method proven over 10 years."</p>
      <p><strong>Which website is the AI more likely to read and share?</strong></p>
      <div class="tip-box">
        <h4>✅ Answer</h4>
        <p><strong>Website B</strong></p>
        <p>AI loves data, facts, and specific methods. It hates marketing slogans.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Schema for Trust -->
  <section id="schema">
    <h2>📐 Pillar 2: Schema for Trust</h2>

    <h3>Why Schema Wins</h3>
    <p>Anyone can write text. Only the site owner can add structured code.</p>

    <h3>Text vs. Schema</h3>
    <pre><code>&lt;!-- ❌ Text (Easy to lie) --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am an expert with 10 years experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;!-- ✅ Schema (Structured fact) --&gt;
&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;
&#123;
  "@type": "Person",
  "name": "Waseem",
  "jobTitle": "English Teacher & Coder",
  "yearsOfExperience": 10
&#125;
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>

    <div class="question-box">
      <h4>🤔 Socratic Question 2</h4>
      <p>You want to prove <strong>Expertise</strong> on your website.</p>
      <p><strong>Option A:</strong> Write "I am an expert" in big bold text.</p>
      <p><strong>Option B:</strong> Add a Schema snippet with your credentials.</p>
      <p><strong>Which one will the AI trust more?</strong></p>
      <div class="tip-box">
        <h4>✅ Answer</h4>
        <p><strong>Option B (Schema)</strong></p>
        <p>Structured data is harder to fake and easier for AI to parse.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- AI-Ready Structure -->
  <section id="structure">
    <h2>📝 Pillar 3: AI-Ready Structure</h2>

    <h3>Q&A Format</h3>
    <p>AI models love <strong>patterns</strong>. They scan pages looking for questions and answers.</p>

    <h3>Bad vs. Good Structure</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>❌ Hard for AI</th>
          <th>✅ Easy for AI</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Long paragraph (500 words)</td>
          <td>Clear Q&A Section</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>No headings</td>
          <td><code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>Question<code>&lt;/h2&gt;</code> + <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>Answer<code>&lt;/p&gt;</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mixed topics</td>
          <td>One topic per section</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="tip-box">
      <h4>💡 Pro Tip</h4>
      <p>Use <strong>FAQ Schema</strong> to mark up your Q&A sections. This tells AI explicitly: "This is a question, and this is the answer."</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question-box">
      <h4>🤔 Socratic Question 3</h4>
      <p>You want AI to quote your advice about "Learning English".</p>
      <p><strong>Option A:</strong> A long paragraph explaining your philosophy.</p>
      <p><strong>Option B:</strong> A clear Q&A Section: <code>&lt;h2&gt;How long does it take?&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;3 months with daily practice.&lt;/p&gt;</code></p>
      <p><strong>Which one is easier for AI to extract?</strong></p>
      <div class="tip-box">
        <h4>✅ Answer</h4>
        <p><strong>Option B</strong></p>
        <p>Think about Google's "People Also Ask" boxes. They love clear Q&A!</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Entity Optimization -->
  <section id="entities">
    <h2>🎯 Entity Optimization</h2>

    <h3>What is an Entity?</h3>
    <p>In the world of AI and search engines, an <strong>entity</strong> is a distinct, well-defined "thing" that can be uniquely identified. Unlike keywords (which are just strings of text), entities have:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Name:</strong> "Waseem" (not just "teacher")</li>
      <li><strong>Type:</strong> Person, Organization, Place, Concept</li>
      <li><strong>Properties:</strong> Job title, location, expertise</li>
      <li><strong>Relationships:</strong> Connected to other entities</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <h4>🔗 Think of entities like a knowledge graph</h4>
      <p>Google's Knowledge Graph connects "Waseem" → "English Teacher" → "Haifa" → "Coding"</p>
      <p>This context helps AI <strong>understand</strong> your content, not just match keywords.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Entity Optimization for GEO</h3>
    <p>When AI models generate answers, they prioritize content with <strong>clear entities</strong>. Here's how to optimize:</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Entity Element</th>
          <th>How to Optimize</th>
          <th>Why AI Loves It</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Person Entity</strong></td>
          <td>Use Person Schema with name, jobTitle, sameAs</td>
          <td>Proves you're real and verifiable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Organization Entity</strong></td>
          <td>Use Organization Schema with logo, address</td>
          <td>Establishes institutional authority</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>SameAs Links</strong></td>
          <td>Link to LinkedIn, GitHub, Twitter profiles</td>
          <td>Confirms identity across platforms</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Knows About</strong></td>
          <td>Schema mentions your expertise areas</td>
          <td>Helps AI match your content to queries</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Example: Person Entity Schema</h3>
    <pre><code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;
&#123;
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  "name": "Waseem",
  "jobTitle": "English Teacher & Full-Stack Developer",
  "url": "https://ward3107.github.io",
  "sameAs": [
    "https://github.com/ward3107",
    "https://linkedin.com/in/waseem",
    "https://twitter.com/waseem"
  ],
  "knowsAbout": [
    "English Teaching",
    "Web Development",
    "SEO and GEO",
    "Technical Writing"
  ],
  "worksFor": &#123;
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "Freelance"
  &#125;,
  "address": &#123;
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    "addressLocality": "Haifa",
    "addressCountry": "Israel"
  &#125;
&#125;
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>

    <div class="key-takeaway-box">
      <h4>🎯 Key Takeaway</h4>
      <p>Entities are the building blocks of AI understanding. When you define entities clearly with Schema markup, you're giving AI a "map" of your content. The better your entities, the more likely AI will quote you accurately.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- AI Overviews & SGE -->
  <section id="ai-overviews">
    <h2>🤖 AI Overviews & SGE: Optimize to Be Cited</h2>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <h4>📅 What changed in 2024–2026?</h4>
      <p>In May 2024, Google rolled out <strong>AI Overviews</strong> (formerly "Search Generative Experience" / SGE) to general US search results, then expanded to 100+ countries by late 2024. The result: a growing share of queries now show an AI-generated answer <em>above</em> the traditional 10 blue links — and that answer cites 3–10 source pages with small chevron links.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:10px"><strong>Source:</strong> <a href="https://blog.google/products/search/generative-ai-google-search-may-2024/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google — "AI Overviews and more" (May 14, 2024)</a></p>
    </div>

    <h3>What an AI Overview actually looks like</h3>
    <p>When you search something like <em>"how to optimize core web vitals for inp"</em>, you may see:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>A boxed, AI-generated paragraph answer at the top.</li>
      <li>3–10 small source cards on the right (or below on mobile) — these are the cited pages.</li>
      <li>Traditional results below.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Being cited in those source cards is the new "ranking #1." A citation gets you brand visibility even on <strong>zero-click</strong> searches.</p>

    <h3>The 6 traits AI Overviews reward</h3>
    <p>Based on research from <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.09735" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aggarwal et al., 2023 ("GEO: Generative Engine Optimization," Princeton/Georgia Tech)</a> and follow-up SEO industry analyses (Ahrefs, Semrush, Surfer 2024–2025), pages that get cited in AI answers tend to share these traits:</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Trait</th>
          <th>Why AI rewards it</th>
          <th>How to implement</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Citations & statistics</strong></td>
          <td>AI prefers verifiable, sourced claims — "+30%+ visibility lift" in the GEO paper</td>
          <td>Cite primary sources inline; include exact numbers, dates, percentages</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Quotation marks around facts</strong></td>
          <td>Easier for the LLM to extract verbatim and attribute</td>
          <td>"INP became a Core Web Vital on March 12, 2024." — exact quotes from named entities</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Authority signals</strong></td>
          <td>Higher trust → higher citation likelihood</td>
          <td>Author byline + Person Schema, .edu/.gov backlinks, recent update date</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Question-form headings</strong></td>
          <td>AI matches user queries to H2/H3 phrasing</td>
          <td>"What is INP?" beats "INP overview"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Fact-dense paragraphs (3–5 sentences)</strong></td>
          <td>Optimal chunk size for retrieval</td>
          <td>Open with the answer, then 2–3 supporting facts, then a closing claim</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>FAQ Schema</strong></td>
          <td>Explicit Q&A pairs are trivial for LLMs to ingest</td>
          <td>Use <code>FAQPage</code> + <code>Question</code> + <code>Answer</code> Schema for common questions</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Real example: dissecting an AI Overview source</h3>
    <p>Here's a pattern that gets cited consistently. Note the structure:</p>

    <pre><code>&lt;h2&gt;What is Interaction to Next Paint (INP)?&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Interaction to Next Paint (INP) is a Core Web Vital
that measures how quickly a page visually responds to
user interactions. Google replaced First Input Delay (FID)
with INP on March 12, 2024 [1].&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A "good" INP is ≤200 milliseconds at the 75th
percentile of user interactions on a page [2]. Above
500 ms is rated "poor."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Common causes of poor INP include long JavaScript
tasks, layout thrashing inside event handlers, and
unoptimized third-party scripts [3].&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sources:&lt;br&gt;
[1] &lt;a href="https://web.dev/blog/inp-cwv-march-12"&gt;
web.dev — INP becomes a Core Web Vital&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
[2] &lt;a href="https://web.dev/articles/inp"&gt;web.dev — INP&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
[3] &lt;a href="https://web.dev/articles/optimize-inp"&gt;web.dev — Optimize INP&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>

    <div class="tip-box">
      <h4>Why this format works</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The H2 mirrors the likely user query.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>The first sentence is the definition</strong> — the LLM can lift it as the answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Each claim is sourced</strong> with a primary citation (web.dev = Google's own publication).</li>
        <li><strong>Specific numbers</strong> (200 ms, 500 ms, March 12, 2024) are easy to verify and harder to hallucinate around.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>The "zero-click problem" — and why citations still matter</h3>
    <p>AI Overviews mean users often don't click your link. So why optimize for them?</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brand exposure:</strong> Your domain appears next to the AI answer. Studies (SparkToro 2024) show ~30% of users do click through when the source is named and recognizable.</li>
      <li><strong>Authority compounding:</strong> Being cited by Google trains other LLMs (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude) — they often crawl Google results to ground their answers.</li>
      <li><strong>Direct LLM citations:</strong> Perplexity, ChatGPT search, and Claude with web tools cite sources visibly with the link prominently displayed.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="key-takeaway-box">
      <h4>🎯 Key Takeaway</h4>
      <p>AI Overviews don't replace SEO — they raise the bar. To be cited, your page needs: <strong>(1)</strong> a question-form heading, <strong>(2)</strong> a direct, factual answer in the first paragraph, <strong>(3)</strong> sourced statistics, and <strong>(4)</strong> recognizable author/organization entities. If you do that, you compound across Google, Perplexity, ChatGPT, and Claude simultaneously.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How LLMs pick sources -->
  <section id="llm-citation">
    <h2>🔬 How LLMs Pick Sources to Quote</h2>

    <p>Different AI products retrieve sources differently. Understanding the mechanism helps you optimize for each.</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Platform</th>
          <th>Retrieval mechanism</th>
          <th>What it favors</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Google AI Overviews</strong></td>
          <td>Uses Google Search's ranking + Gemini synthesis. Pulls from top-ranked, high-E-E-A-T pages.</td>
          <td>High-authority domains, recent content, fact-density, Knowledge Graph entities</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Perplexity</strong></td>
          <td>Real-time web search per query. Cites 4–8 sources visibly with footnotes.</td>
          <td>Recency, clear question-answer structure, primary sources, .edu/.gov</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>ChatGPT Search</strong></td>
          <td>Bing search + GPT-4 synthesis. Increasingly cites sources inline.</td>
          <td>Bing's index, structured content, clear authority</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Claude (with web tools)</strong></td>
          <td>Tool-call to search APIs; quotes verbatim from sources when relevant.</td>
          <td>Verbatim-quotable phrasing, named entities, clear definitions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>ChatGPT (no search)</strong></td>
          <td>Pre-training data only (knowledge cutoff). Won't cite recent content.</td>
          <td>Long-standing web content, encyclopedic phrasing, classic Wikipedia-style facts</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>The 5 universal "citation triggers"</h3>
    <p>From the GEO paper and observed citation patterns:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Numerical specificity</strong> — "75% of recipes use butter" beats "most recipes use butter."</li>
      <li><strong>Named authority quotes</strong> — "According to Google's web.dev team, ..." outperforms unattributed claims.</li>
      <li><strong>Recent dates</strong> — content updated within 12 months gets cited 2-3× more often for time-sensitive topics.</li>
      <li><strong>Direct definitions in the first 100 words</strong> — LLMs lift the opening for "what is X" queries.</li>
      <li><strong>Outbound citations</strong> — paradoxically, pages that <em>cite others</em> are themselves cited more often (signals trustworthiness).</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="reflect-box">
      <h4>🤔 Reflection</h4>
      <p>Pick any page on your site. Read the first paragraph aloud. Could an AI lift it as a complete, accurate answer to a likely user question? If not, rewrite it: lead with the answer, then add 2–3 sourced supporting facts.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- E-E-A-T Framework -->
  <section id="eeat">
    <h2>🛡️ E-E-A-T: The Trust Framework</h2>

    <p>Google and AI use <strong>E-E-A-T</strong> to decide if they should trust you:</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Letter</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
          <th>How to Show It in Code</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>E</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Experience</strong></td>
          <td>Case studies, project logs, before/after data</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>E</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Expertise</strong></td>
          <td>Author Schema, credentials, qualifications</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>A</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Authoritativeness</strong></td>
          <td>Backlinks, citations, mentions by others</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>T</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Trustworthiness</strong></td>
          <td>HTTPS, clear contact info, accurate Schema</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="question-box">
      <h4>🤔 Socratic Question 4</h4>
      <p>You write an article: <em>"Coding Helps Learn English Faster."</em></p>
      <p><strong>To make AI trust this claim, what should you add?</strong></p>
      <ol>
        <li>Nothing (Just your opinion).</li>
        <li>A link to a university study + Your Author Schema.</li>
        <li>More exclamation marks!!!</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="tip-box">
        <h4>✅ Answer</h4>
        <p><strong>#2 (Link + Schema)</strong></p>
        <p>This proves you aren't just making it up. AI loves citations!</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <h4>📅 Why two E's? (the 2022 update)</h4>
      <p>The framework was originally <strong>E-A-T</strong>. Google added the second <strong>"E" for Experience</strong> on <strong>December 15, 2022</strong>, in response to AI-generated content flooding the web. <em>Experience</em> means: did the author actually do/use/visit the thing? A medical review by a doctor who has seen patients beats an AI-generated summary, even if the summary is factually correct.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:10px"><strong>Source:</strong> <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2022/12/google-raters-guidelines-e-e-a-t" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Search Central — "Our latest update to the quality rater guidelines: E-E-A-T"</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Operationalizing E-E-A-T -->
  <section id="eeat-operationalize">
    <h2>🧰 Operationalizing E-E-A-T (a concrete 14-step playbook)</h2>

    <p>Most courses explain what E-E-A-T <em>is</em>. Here's what to actually <em>do</em>, mapped to each letter. Treat this as a checklist for any new page.</p>

    <h3>Experience (you've actually done it)</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Add an "About the author" box</strong> at the top of long articles with a real photo, role, and 1-sentence "why I wrote this."</li>
      <li><strong>Show your work</strong> — screenshots, dashboards, before/after numbers from real projects. A Lighthouse screenshot beats a paragraph about Lighthouse.</li>
      <li><strong>First-person experience signals</strong> in prose: "I migrated 12 sites from FID to INP-friendly architecture in 2024..." carries more weight than "to optimize INP, you should..."</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Expertise (you know the topic deeply)</h3>
    <ol start="4">
      <li><strong>Author page</strong> with credentials, certifications, education, years of experience — and link it from every article via the <code>Article.author</code> Schema property.</li>
      <li><strong>Person Schema</strong> with <code>hasCredential</code>, <code>alumniOf</code>, <code>knowsAbout</code> populated.</li>
      <li><strong>Sub-topic depth</strong> — for a topic like "INP," cover related sub-topics (TBT, long tasks, scheduler.yield). Topical authority is a measurable signal.</li>
      <li><strong>Internal linking to related expert content</strong> on your own site (creates a topic cluster).</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Authoritativeness (others recognize you)</h3>
    <ol start="8">
      <li><strong>Author bio links to public profiles</strong> — LinkedIn, GitHub, ORCID, conference talks. Use <code>sameAs</code> in Person Schema.</li>
      <li><strong>Get cited by recognized sources</strong> — guest posts, podcasts, expert quotes (HARO/Connectively, expert roundups). Each backlink from a recognized authority compounds.</li>
      <li><strong>Wikipedia / Wikidata presence</strong> (if eligible) — the strongest entity-disambiguation signal Google has.</li>
      <li><strong>Press / media mentions</strong> — even unlinked mentions count as entity signals in Google's Knowledge Graph.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Trustworthiness (the foundation — most important per Google)</h3>
    <ol start="12">
      <li><strong>HTTPS, valid SSL, no mixed content</strong>. Run <a href="https://www.ssllabs.com/ssltest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qualys SSL Labs</a> — aim for A or A+.</li>
      <li><strong>Visible contact info</strong>: real address, phone, email. Add <code>ContactPage</code> Schema. Build trust like a real business.</li>
      <li><strong>Editorial transparency</strong>: publication date, last-updated date, sources cited, corrections policy. The Healthline / NIH / Mayo Clinic format is the gold standard — copy it.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="success-box">
      <h4>🎯 The 5-minute E-E-A-T audit</h4>
      <p>Open any article you've written. Can you answer "yes" to all five?</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Is the author's name + photo + credential visible above the fold?</li>
        <li>Is the publication date AND last-updated date visible?</li>
        <li>Is at least one external primary source linked in the first 500 words?</li>
        <li>Is there a "How we tested / how we know" sentence somewhere on the page?</li>
        <li>Does the author have a public profile that anyone can verify (LinkedIn, ORCID)?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If you can answer yes to all five, you've already beaten 80% of competing pages.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Code example: Article + Person Schema with full E-E-A-T signals</h3>
    <pre><code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;
